    {
      "id": "k2hvxph",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "10.5 Praja Mandal Movement"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Praja Mandals were people's organizations in princely states demanding democratic rights. Important Praja Mandals in Rajasthan:",
      "id": "rm9ktev"
    },
    {
      "id": "r8p17w2",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Marwar Praja Mandal (1934): Founded by Jai Narayan Vyas and Abhay Mal Jain",
        "Mewar Praja Mandal (1938): Founded by Manikya Lal Verma",
        "Jaipur Praja Mandal (1936): Founded by Kapoor Chand Patni",
        "Bikaner Praja Mandal (1936): Founded by Rishi Dutt Mehta",
        "Alwar Praja Mandal (1938): Founded by Harinath Shastri",
        "Hadoti Praja Mandal: Founded by Nayan Verma"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "tzqebz1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "10.6 Integration of Rajasthan"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "After independence, 19 princely states were merged to form Rajasthan in seven phases under Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel (with P. Menon as Secretary):",
      "id": "frlj8jh"
    },
    {
      "id": "wntc6kb",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Phase</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Date</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Formation</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Phase 1 (Matsya Union)</p>",
            "<p>18 March 1948</p>",
            "<p>Alwar, Bharatpur, Dhaulpur, Karauli</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Phase 2 (Rajasthan Union)</p>",
            "<p>25 March 1948</p>",
            "<p>Banswara, Bundi, Dungarpur, Jhalawar, Kishangarh, Kota, Pratapgarh, Shahpura, Tonk</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Phase 3 (United Rajasthan)</p>",
            "<p>18 April 1948</p>",
            "<p>Mewar (Udaipur) joined Phase 2</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Phase 4 (Greater Rajasthan)</p>",
            "<p>30 March 1949</p>",
            "<p>Bikaner, Jodhpur, Jaipur, Jaisalmer joined</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Phase 5 (United Greater Rajasthan)</p>",
            "<p>15 May 1949</p>",
            "<p>Matsya Union merged</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Phase 6</p>",
            "<p>26 January 1950</p>",
            "<p>Sirohi partially merged</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Phase 7 (Complete Rajasthan)</p>",
            "<p>1 November 1956</p>",
            "<p>Ajmer and Abu Taluka merged; Current form</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "30 March 1949 is celebrated as Rajasthan Diwas (Foundation Day of Greater Rajasthan). Hiralal Shastri was the first Chief Minister of Rajasthan (1949-1951). The first elected Chief Minister was Tika Ram Paliwal. Sardar Patel is called the 'Iron Man of India' and is credited with integration.",
      "id": "6lljnar"
    }
  ],
